  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A roof doesn't need much from you, but it does need attention at the right times. In Temple, those times line up with the weather: spring hail and wind, a long hot summer, fall leaves and a second round of storms, and the occasional hard freeze. This roof maintenance checklist breaks the year into those seasons and sticks to checks you can do safely from your yard, your driveway, or a safely accessible attic. It also covers what to leave to a professional and how to keep records that pay off if you ever need a repair, a claim, or a sale.</p>

<div class="callout callout--warn"><strong>Ground rules:</strong> None of these checks require climbing onto the roof, and you shouldn't. A pair of binoculars, a phone camera with zoom, a flashlight, and a sturdy ladder for gutter-level or attic access are all you need. If something looks wrong up top, take a photo and call a roofer.</div>

<h2>Your Basic Tool Kit</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Binoculars or a zoom camera</strong> for looking at shingles, vents, and flashing from the ground.</li>
  <li><strong>A bright flashlight or headlamp</strong> for the attic.</li>
  <li><strong>A notebook or a phone folder</strong> dedicated to the roof, so photos and notes stay together.</li>
  <li><strong>Gloves and a dust mask</strong> if you'll be in the attic around insulation.</li>
  <li><strong>A garden hose</strong> to check that downspouts flow freely from ground level.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Seasonal Roof Maintenance Checklist for Central Texas</h2>
<p>Central Texas roofs face different stresses as the year turns, so a single annual glance isn't enough. Here's what to look at, season by season.</p>

<h3>Late Winter: Before Spring Storm Season</h3>
<p>Spring severe weather in Bell County commonly ramps up around March and can run into June. The weeks before that are the best time to find small problems, because a minor weakness is much easier to fix before hail and high winds test it.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Walk the perimeter and scan each roof slope with binoculars for missing, curled, or cracked shingles.</li>
  <li>Look at pipe boots and roof vents for cracked rubber, rust, or anything sitting crooked.</li>
  <li>Check that gutters are attached firmly, aren't sagging, and are clear from winter debris.</li>
  <li>Trim tree limbs that hang over or rub against the roof. Hire an arborist for large limbs.</li>
  <li>In the attic, look for daylight around penetrations and any water stains on the decking.</li>
  <li>Take a fresh set of "baseline" photos of every side of the roof. If a storm hits later, you'll have a before-and-after comparison.</li>
</ul>

<h3>After Any Major Storm</h3>
<p>After hail or strong winds, do a quick check once it's safe to go outside. Stay away from downed power lines and wait until lightning has passed.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Look for shingles or pieces of ridge cap on the ground, and debris lying on the roof.</li>
  <li>Check soft metal like gutters, downspouts, AC fins, and mailbox tops for fresh dents. They often show hail impacts more clearly than shingles do.</li>
  <li>Scan for lifted or creased shingles, especially along edges and ridges.</li>
  <li>Check ceilings and the attic for new stains or drips over the following days.</li>
  <li>Photograph everything with the date, then use our <a href="/tools/storm-damage-checklist/">storm damage checklist</a> to keep notes organized.</li>
</ul>
<p>If anything looks off, a professional inspection is the next step. Hail damage in particular is hard to judge from the ground, and our guides on <a href="/blog/how-to-spot-hail-damage-on-your-roof-temple-tx/">spotting hail damage</a> and <a href="/blog/wind-damage-roof-bell-county/">what to do after wind damage</a> explain why.</p>

<h3>Summer: Heat and UV</h3>
<p>Temple summers regularly push into the upper 90s and past 100°F, with strong sun for months. Heat is less dramatic than hail, but it steadily ages shingles, sealants, and rubber components.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Check the attic in the early morning, not the afternoon, when it's cooler and safer.</li>
  <li>Make sure soffit vents aren't blocked by insulation or paint, and that ridge or roof vents look intact from the ground.</li>
  <li>Confirm bathroom and kitchen exhaust fans vent outside, not into the attic.</li>
  <li>Look for signs of heat aging from the ground: shingles that look blistered, curled at the edges, or noticeably faded on the sunniest slopes.</li>
  <li>Watch for granules collecting at downspout outlets, a sign shingles are wearing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ventilation has a big effect on how long shingles last in our heat. Our article on <a href="/blog/texas-summer-heat-roof-attic-ventilation/">summer heat and attic ventilation</a> explains what good airflow looks like.</p>

<h3>Fall: Leaves and the Secondary Storm Season</h3>
<p>Central Texas often sees a second, smaller storm season in the fall, and that's also when leaves and pecan debris pile up in gutters and valleys.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Clear gutters from a properly set ladder at the eaves, or hire it out. Don't walk the roof to reach them.</li>
  <li>Run a hose into each downspout from the ground to confirm water exits freely and drains away from the foundation.</li>
  <li>Look at roof valleys from the ground for piled-up leaves that can dam water.</li>
  <li>Check that downspout extensions or splash blocks are in place. Bell County's expansive clay soils don't respond well to water pooling against the slab.</li>
  <li>Re-scan the roof after any fall storms, just as you did in spring.</li>
</ul>

<!--cta-->

<h3>Winter: Freeze Prep</h3>
<p>Hard freezes aren't frequent here, but the February 2021 winter storm showed how unprepared many homes were. A few quick checks help:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Make sure attic insulation is evenly spread and not pulled away from exterior walls.</li>
  <li>Check that pipes in the attic are insulated, since a burst pipe can look a lot like a roof leak.</li>
  <li>Look for frost or damp spots on the underside of the decking on cold mornings, which can signal a ventilation or condensation issue rather than a roof leak.</li>
  <li>After ice or sleet, watch for heavy icicles on gutters and keep people clear of the eaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Quick-Reference Roof Maintenance Checklist Table</h2>
<p>Print this or save it on your phone. Each item is a ground-level or attic check unless noted.</p>
