    <tr><td>June–August</td><td>Granules at downspouts</td><td>Ground</td><td>Heavy granule buildup</td></tr>
    <tr><td>October–November</td><td>Gutters, downspouts, valleys</td><td>Ladder at eaves, ground</td><td>Overflow, standing water, packed leaves</td></tr>
    <tr><td>December–January</td><td>Insulation, attic pipes, condensation</td><td>Attic</td><td>Frost or dampness on decking</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Every 1–2 years</td><td>Professional inspection</td><td>Roofer</td><td>Anything you noted during the year</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>

<h2>Adjusting the Checklist for Your Home</h2>
<p>Every house is different, so tune the list to what you actually have overhead.</p>
<h3>Older Homes Near Downtown Temple</h3>
<p>Many older homes in central Temple have mature trees, more complex rooflines, and roofs that may have been re-covered over the years. Give extra attention to chimney flashing, the spots where additions meet the original structure, and gutters that have been rehung more than once. In the attic, look for older plank decking with gaps, past water staining, and wiring or ductwork that has been added over time.</p>
<h3>Newer Subdivisions in West and South Temple</h3>
<p>Newer homes often have younger roofs and fewer trees, so leaves are less of a concern. Wind exposure can be greater on open lots, though, and builder-grade components like pipe boots and vents may need attention sooner than the shingles do. Keep a close eye on edges and ridges after strong winds.</p>
<h3>Homes With Heavy Tree Cover</h3>
<p>Wooded lots, including many around Lake Belton, deal with more leaf buildup, branch impacts, and shade that keeps parts of the roof damp longer. Plan on clearing gutters more than once in the fall, watch shaded slopes for moss or algae streaks, and keep limbs trimmed back from the roof.</p>

<h2>What to Leave to a Professional</h2>
<p>Homeowners can spot most early warning signs, but some work belongs to someone with the right equipment and training:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Anything on the roof surface.</strong> Replacing shingles, resealing flashing, swapping pipe boots, or clearing valleys by hand all require walking the roof.</li>
  <li><strong>Close-up hail and wind assessment.</strong> Bruised shingles and broken seal strips often can't be seen from the ground.</li>
  <li><strong>Leak tracing.</strong> Water travels before it drips, so finding the true source takes experience. Our article on <a href="/blog/common-causes-of-roof-leaks-central-texas/">common causes of roof leaks</a> explains where to start.</li>
  <li><strong>Tarping after damage.</strong> A loose tarp can do more harm than good in the next wind.</li>
  <li><strong>Gutter repair and re-pitching.</strong> Sagging or poorly sloped gutters often need re-hanging rather than cleaning. See our <a href="/services/gutters-roof-drainage-temple-tx/">gutters and roof drainage</a> service.</li>
</ul>
<p>A periodic <a href="/services/roof-inspection-temple-tx/">professional roof inspection</a> fills in what you can't see from below. How often depends on the roof's age and recent weather; our article on <a href="/blog/how-often-roof-inspection-temple-tx/">how often to have your roof inspected</a> covers that. If your checks turn up a specific issue, our <a href="/services/roof-repair-temple-tx/">roof repair</a> team can handle it before it becomes a leak.</p>

<h2>Record-Keeping Tips That Pay Off Later</h2>
<p>Good records make every future conversation about your roof easier, whether it's with a roofer, an insurance adjuster, or a buyer.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Keep one folder.</strong> A physical file or a cloud folder named for the roof works. Put photos, invoices, warranty paperwork, and inspection reports in it.</li>
  <li><strong>Date your photos.</strong> Take them from the same spots each time, such as each corner of the yard, so changes are easy to compare.</li>
  <li><strong>Log the roof's history.</strong> Note the install year, the material, the installer, and any repairs with dates and what was done.</li>
  <li><strong>Write down storm dates.</strong> If hail or high winds hit your neighborhood, note the date even if you don't see damage. It helps if problems surface later.</li>
  <li><strong>Save manufacturer documents.</strong> Shingle warranties often have registration and maintenance conditions, so keep the paperwork and read the terms.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><strong>Tip:</strong> Baseline photos taken before storm season are useful if you ever file a claim. Our <a href="/services/roof-insurance-claim-assistance-temple-tx/">insurance claim assistance</a> page explains how we document damage. We're roofers, not public adjusters, so the claim itself stays between you and your insurer.</div>

<h2>Building the Habit</h2>
<p>A roof maintenance checklist only works if it becomes routine. Tie each season's checks to something you already do, such as changing HVAC filters or clearing the yard after a storm, and keep your notes in one place. Small fixes caught early are almost always cheaper than the damage a slow leak causes, and they help a roof reach the end of its expected life. If you'd like a professional set of eyes before storm season or after a rough one, <a href="/free-roof-inspection/">schedule a free roof inspection</a> with Temple Roofers. We'll photograph what we find and give you a written summary you can add to your records.</p>
